; ======================================================================
;  memmap.pi3 - the Raspberry Pi 3 monitor's memory map, and the seam the
;               shared core asks it through.
;
;  Anvil/Core/memrange.pbi refuses a range that would land on the running
;  monitor and offers the windows a payload may live in; it knows no
;  addresses of its own and walks whatever the board enumerates. This is
;  this board's enumeration. Its counterpart is
;  RaspberryPi4/Board/memmap.pi4, which is far longer because that board
;  carries a boot trail, a screenshot area, a DSI framebuffer and a
;  module arena; none of those exist here yet, and a region declared for
;  something that does not exist would refuse addresses for no reason.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  WHERE EVERYTHING IS, AND WHO DECIDED
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The direct `pi3` build fixes the monitor image/BSS/stack addresses in
;  board.pi3 and the linker invocation. The firmware supplies the DTB
;  pointer in x0; framebuffer scanout is also firmware-allocated. This
;  file records the monitor's reserved ranges and legal payload windows.
;
;    $00000000..$000FFFFF   the firmware's own low memory and the ARM
;                           spin table. Not ours and not mapped as ours.
;    $00200000..           THE MONITOR'S CODE. Firmware loads kernel8.img
;                           here; the end is measured from the image.
;    firmware x0..x0+size  THE DEVICE TREE. Its validated live range is
;                           protected below; $01000000 is only the fallback
;                           range before firmware x0 has been captured.
;    $01100000..$01DFFFFF   the monitor's variables. BSS_ADDRESS.
;    $01E00000..$01EFFFFF   the monitor's stack, growing DOWN from
;                           STACK_ADDRESS. One megabyte.
;    $01F10000..$01F12FFF   the page tables. #MMU3_TABLE_BASE.
;    $01FF0000..$01FFFFFF   the legacy updater handoff reservation.
;    $02000000..$02DFFFFF   the legacy update staging reservation. It
;                           remains protected from payload placement.
;    $02E00000..$02FFFFFF   low payload window, ending before the fixed
;                           display backbuffer at $03000000.
;    $03000000..$038C9FFF   the 9,216,000-byte offscreen display buffer.
;                           DMA copies completed render work to scanout.
;    $03A00000..            high payload window. The gap through
;                           $039FFFFF is excluded because the 2 MiB NC
;                           page-table granularity covers the buffer.
;                           The window ends at firmware-reported ARM RAM.
;    $3F000000..$3FFFFFFF   the peripherals. Not memory.
;
;  EACH PAYLOAD WINDOW'S TOP IS READ, NOT WRITTEN DOWN. pi3_ram_end comes
;  from the firmware's "get ARM memory" property tag during boot, and it
;  is BELOW the fitted size by whatever gpu_mem takes. A window that
;  ended at a constant would offer an operator memory the VideoCore owns
;  on a board configured with a larger split, and the failure would be a
;  payload that loads and then behaves oddly - the worst kind.
;
;  THESE ARE TWO WINDOWS AROUND MONITOR-OWNED DISPLAY MEMORY, not two
;  separate DRAM banks. The BCM2837 has no memory above its peripheral
;  aperture; both windows stay below $3F000000.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  DEPENDENCIES: include after RaspberryPi3/Lib/mmu.pi3 (#MMU3_TABLE_BASE,
;  #MMU3_TABLE_BYTES) and after the A/B module (#PI3_UPDATE_HANDOFF,
;  pi3_ram_end). Include BEFORE Anvil/Core/state.pbi, which is where
;  #CRC_POLY comes from on every board. House rule 10: this file includes
;  nothing itself.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE GRAIN IS ONE MEGABYTE.
;
;  The monitor's code region is the live image rounded UP to a megabyte,
;  and the payload window starts at the next megabyte above the staging
;  arena. Rounding is what keeps a payload's load address stable across
;  builds: without it, every rebuild that changed the image's size by one
;  byte would move the address a host tool was told to use.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#MON_GRAIN = $100000

#MON_LO        = $200000        ; the slot contract's LOAD_ADDRESS
#MON_DATA_LO   = $1100000       ; BSS_ADDRESS
#MON_DATA_HI   = $1DFFFFF       ; one below the stack region
#MON_STACK     = $1F00000       ; STACK_ADDRESS - the TOP; it grows down
#MON_STACK_LO  = $1E00000       ; one megabyte of stack, reserved
#MON_STACK_HI  = $1EFFFFF
#MON_DTB_LO    = $1000000       ; fallback before firmware x0 is captured
#MON_DTB_HI    = $10FFFFF       ; live range uses pi3_dtb/pi3_dtb_end
#MON_HANDOFF_LO = $1FF0000      ; #PI3_UPDATE_HANDOFF
#MON_HANDOFF_HI = $1FFFFFF
#MON_TABLES_LO = $1F10000       ; #MMU3_TABLE_BASE
#MON_TABLES_HI = $1F12FFF       ; + #MMU3_TABLE_BYTES - 1
#MON_STAGE_LO  = $2000000       ; the A/B staging arena, as the loader
#MON_STAGE_HI  = $2DFFFFF       ; configures it: base $2000000, $E00000
#MON_FB_RENDER_LO = $3000000    ; fixed-size, NC display render backbuffer
#MON_FB_RENDER_HI = $38C9FFF    ; 9,216,000 bytes; source for DMA scanout

; The payload window's floor. Everything the boot machinery owns is
; below it, by construction and not by luck.
#PAY0_LO = $2E00000
#PAY1_LO = $3A00000             ; first 2 MiB block after render NC coverage

; Its ceiling when the firmware would not say how much memory the ARM
; has. $3EFFFFFF is the last byte below the peripheral aperture, so this
; is the largest value that could ever be right; HwPayHi() clamps to the
; firmware's answer whenever there is one.
#PAY0_HI_MAX = $3EFFFFFF

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The region indices, named so that the Select blocks below and any
;  reader of `map` agree about what region 4 is.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#MON_REGION_CODE    = 0
#MON_REGION_DATA    = 1
#MON_REGION_STACK   = 2
#MON_REGION_TABLES  = 3
#MON_REGION_DTB     = 4
#MON_REGION_HANDOFF = 5
#MON_REGION_STAGE   = 6
#MON_REGION_FB_RENDER = 7
#MON_REGION_FB_SCANOUT = 8
#MON_REGION_COUNT   = 9

; ======================================================================
;  WHERE THIS IMAGE ACTUALLY IS - measured from the linker's own symbols.
;
;  The slot contract pins the load address, so on this board these will
;  answer $200000 and the image end every time. They are still READ
;  rather than written down, for two reasons that both cost nothing:
;  a monitor built for a bench experiment at another address reports
;  itself correctly, and HwMonBytes() below becomes the size of the image
;  rather than a number somebody has to remember to update.
;
;  THE ASM WRITES TO A GLOBAL AND THE PROCEDURE RETURNS IT. An ASM block
;  cannot hand a value to the expression around it, so the read must come
;  back through memory. Each procedure has its own slot: these are
;  link-time constants, the same bytes on every call, so separate slots
;  cost eight bytes and remove a question.
;
;  x16 and x17 are the scratch pair the emitted preamble itself uses, so
;  neither can be live across an ASM block here.
; ======================================================================
Global monimage_lo.i = 0
Global monimage_end.i = 0

Procedure.i MonImageLo()
  ASM
    adrp x16, __image_start__
    add  x16, x16, #:lo12:__image_start__
    adrp x17, global_monimage_lo
    add  x17, x17, #:lo12:global_monimage_lo
    str  x16, [x17]
  EndASM
  ProcedureReturn monimage_lo
EndProcedure

; ONE PAST the last byte, which is the end the linker states.
Procedure.i MonImageEnd()
  ASM
    adrp x16, __image_end__
    add  x16, x16, #:lo12:__image_end__
    adrp x17, global_monimage_end
    add  x17, x17, #:lo12:global_monimage_end
    str  x16, [x17]
  EndASM
  ProcedureReturn monimage_end
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwMonBytes()
  ProcedureReturn MonImageEnd() - MonImageLo()
EndProcedure

; Round UP to the next multiple of #MON_GRAIN. A divide rather than a
; mask because every address handed to it is a positive .i well inside 63
; bits, and the divide says what it means.
Procedure.i MonGrainUp(a.i)
  ProcedureReturn ((a + #MON_GRAIN - 1) / #MON_GRAIN) * #MON_GRAIN
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwMonLo()
  ProcedureReturn MonImageLo()
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwMonHi()
  ProcedureReturn MonGrainUp(MonImageEnd()) - 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwMonStack()
  ProcedureReturn #MON_STACK
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  EVERY REGION THIS MONITOR OWNS.
;
;  HitsMonitor() in Anvil/Core/memrange.pbi walks these and refuses any
;  command whose range touches one. The list contains fixed monitor
;  ranges, the live firmware DTB, and display buffers:
;
;    THE DEVICE TREE is region 4 at the range validated from firmware x0.
;    A `fill` over it could corrupt the boot description still used by
;    the monitor, so its live range stays reserved.
;
;    THE HANDOFF RECORD is region 5, retained for the separate legacy
;    recovery/update path.
;
;    THE STAGING ARENA is region 6, retained for recovery/update tools.
;
;  THE ORDER IS DELIBERATE. Region 0 is the code, because it is the one
;  an operator hits by aiming a payload at the load address.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i HwMonRegions()
  ProcedureReturn #MON_REGION_COUNT
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwMonRegionLo(i.i)
  Select i
    Case #MON_REGION_CODE    : ProcedureReturn HwMonLo()
    Case #MON_REGION_DATA    : ProcedureReturn #MON_DATA_LO
    Case #MON_REGION_STACK   : ProcedureReturn #MON_STACK_LO
    Case #MON_REGION_TABLES  : ProcedureReturn #MON_TABLES_LO
    Case #MON_REGION_DTB
      If pi3_dtb>0 And pi3_dtb_end>pi3_dtb : ProcedureReturn pi3_dtb : EndIf
      ProcedureReturn #MON_DTB_LO
    Case #MON_REGION_HANDOFF : ProcedureReturn #MON_HANDOFF_LO
    Case #MON_REGION_STAGE   : ProcedureReturn #MON_STAGE_LO
    Case #MON_REGION_FB_RENDER : ProcedureReturn #MON_FB_RENDER_LO
    Case #MON_REGION_FB_SCANOUT : ProcedureReturn Pi3FbScanoutBase()
  EndSelect
  ; A region index this board does not have. An address here would make
  ; HitsMonitor() refuse a range for a region that does not exist; an
  ; empty range - lo above hi - matches nothing, which is the only answer
  ; that cannot invent a refusal.
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwMonRegionHi(i.i)
  Select i
    Case #MON_REGION_CODE    : ProcedureReturn HwMonHi()
    Case #MON_REGION_DATA    : ProcedureReturn #MON_DATA_HI
    Case #MON_REGION_STACK   : ProcedureReturn #MON_STACK_HI
    Case #MON_REGION_TABLES  : ProcedureReturn #MON_TABLES_HI
    Case #MON_REGION_DTB
      If pi3_dtb>0 And pi3_dtb_end>pi3_dtb : ProcedureReturn pi3_dtb_end-1 : EndIf
      ProcedureReturn #MON_DTB_HI
    Case #MON_REGION_HANDOFF : ProcedureReturn #MON_HANDOFF_HI
    Case #MON_REGION_STAGE   : ProcedureReturn #MON_STAGE_HI
    Case #MON_REGION_FB_RENDER : ProcedureReturn #MON_FB_RENDER_HI
    Case #MON_REGION_FB_SCANOUT
      If Pi3FbScanoutBase()<>0 And Pi3FbSize()>0 : ProcedureReturn Pi3FbScanoutBase()+Pi3FbSize()-1 : EndIf
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; A short phrase naming the region, printed by `map` beside its
; addresses. A printing procedure rather than a returned string because
; the board owns the prose about its own hardware and the core owns the
; layout of the line.
Procedure HwMonRegionSay(i.i)
  Select i
    Case #MON_REGION_CODE
      Print("this monitor's code, measured from the image")
    Case #MON_REGION_DATA
      Print("its variables, placed by the slot contract")
    Case #MON_REGION_STACK
      Print("its stack, growing down from the top of this range")
    Case #MON_REGION_TABLES
      Print("the page tables the MMU is translating through")
    Case #MON_REGION_DTB
      Print("the device tree, where the firmware and the loader pinned it")
    Case #MON_REGION_HANDOFF
      Print("the loader's A/B handoff record")
    Case #MON_REGION_STAGE
      Print("the A/B staging arena a serial update reads into")
    Case #MON_REGION_FB_RENDER
      Print("the offscreen display backbuffer used by DMA")
    Case #MON_REGION_FB_SCANOUT
      Print("the firmware-owned HDMI scanout surface")
    Default
      Print("a region this board does not have")
  EndSelect
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE PAYLOAD WINDOW.
;
;  ONE window, and its ceiling is the firmware's answer. pi3_ram_end is
;  set by Pi3BootMemory() from the "get ARM memory" property tag before
;  the MMU comes on; it is one past the last byte the ARM was given.
;  Until it has been set it is 0, and the window is then reported at its
;  largest possible extent rather than as empty - an empty window would
;  make `receive` refuse everywhere during boot, which is exactly when a
;  recovery upload is most wanted.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i HwPayWindows()
  ; Never advertise a payload range beyond a known firmware RAM limit.
  ; Before discovery, keep the conservative maximum recovery windows.
  If pi3_ram_end>0 And pi3_ram_end<=#PAY0_LO : ProcedureReturn 0 : EndIf
  If pi3_ram_end>0 And pi3_ram_end<=#PAY1_LO : ProcedureReturn 1 : EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 2
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwPayLo(i.i)
  If i = 0
    ProcedureReturn #PAY0_LO
  ElseIf i = 1
    ProcedureReturn #PAY1_LO
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwPayHi(i.i)
  If i < 0 Or i > 1
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If i=0
    If pi3_ram_end>0 And pi3_ram_end<=#MON_FB_RENDER_LO : ProcedureReturn pi3_ram_end-1 : EndIf
    ProcedureReturn #MON_FB_RENDER_LO-1
  EndIf
  If pi3_ram_end>0
    If pi3_ram_end<=#PAY1_LO : ProcedureReturn #PAY1_LO-1 : EndIf
    If pi3_ram_end-1<#PAY0_HI_MAX : ProcedureReturn pi3_ram_end-1 : EndIf
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn #PAY0_HI_MAX
EndProcedure

; A version-2 payload declares only its stack top. Admission checks the
; initial 16-byte ABI stack area inside the DRAM window available to
; payloads; the PMF format does not describe later stack growth.
Procedure.i HwPmfStackAllowed(lo.i, hi.i)
  Define w.i
  Define r.i
  Define regionCount.i
  If lo<0 Or hi<lo
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  For w=0 To HwPayWindows()-1
    If lo>=HwPayLo(w) And hi<=HwPayHi(w)
      ; Dynamic firmware scanout is also monitor-owned. Do not allow a
      ; payload's initial stack to land there even though the interval is
      ; otherwise inside the reported RAM window.
      regionCount=HwMonRegions()
      For r=0 To regionCount-1
        If lo<=HwMonRegionHi(r) And hi>=HwMonRegionLo(r)
          ProcedureReturn 0
        EndIf
      Next
      ProcedureReturn 1
    EndIf
  Next
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwStageAddr() - where a host should put a file it is sending.
;
;  The bottom of the payload window and nothing more: the first address
;  clear of everything the boot arrangement owns, in the window every
;  refusal already prints. Host tools ask for this rather than carrying a
;  constant of their own, which is how a tool learns that the monitor
;  grew.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwStageAddr()
  ProcedureReturn HwPayLo(0)
EndProcedure
